  "query_preview": "..High pressure is centered over the four corners\ntoday. This is drawing up moisture into the region with PWATs\naround 1.1\" across southwestern Utah and west central Utah and \nupwards of 0.9\" extending into eastern and northern Utah. This\nwill result in diurnal convection developing each day and\npersisting through the early evening before weakening and\ndissipating overnight. Potential for nocturnal convection will \nbe present with any convection that develops into an MCS-ish \nfeatures, but guidance usually struggles with representing the \ndetails of this activity until the day of. WPC ERO shows a \nmarginal risk for flash flooding across the southern 2/3rds of \nUtah through Wednesday before spreading into all of Utah Thursday \nand Friday thanks to an approaching trough.\n\nThis trough will be moving into the western CONUS during the\nsecond half of the week. This will help to develop more widespread\nconvection across northern Utah and SW Wyoming as well as cool\ntemperatures 10-15 degrees compared to where they have been.\nHighs on Thursday and Friday are forecast to be in the low 80s\nacross most valley locations with low 90s across lower Washington\ncounty. As this trough approaches southwesterly winds will\nincrease, but the pressure gradient remains weak which will limit\nthe magnitude of these southwesterly winds to 20-30 mph across the\nsouthwester portions of the state.\n\nAs this trough continues to push through Great Basin it will\npush the ridge to the south/southeastern CONUS. Drier air will\nadvect into the area and decrease the flash flood risk as storms\nbecome more isolated and high based in nature.",
